{"schema":"vqsv-webnative-battle-status-mechanics-v1","balanceVersion":"unified-v5","scanLimit":4096,"note":"buffRules are indexed by buff id and carry what the producer stores on the unit: the duration in rounds, the primary and secondary magnitudes, the stack cap and how many actions the status owns before an action consumes it instead of the round-start pass ticking it down. Identity, kind, tick phase, refresh policy and consume policy for every status live in battle/statuses.json, and the debuff magnitudes plus the skills that put a debuff on a target live in battle/debuff-producers.json. This file adds the buff magnitudes, the source skills that change them, the value each buff actually stores on the unit when it is applied, the post-skill route that says which skill applies which buff to whom once it lands and with what roll the runtime gates the leech it carries, the extra power the four conditional debuff skills hit with, the self-hit chance of the two action-control debuffs, and the status ids the round-start pass animates per bank. buffStoredValues is a declaration, not a dump: the engine writes those magnitudes in a switch of its own, so before the file is written every row is replayed against that switch over the grid named in buffStoredValueVerification, replayed again on a unit whose projected speed and defense have been moved off its bases to prove every percent reads a base stat, and then separated: the grid has to hold a unit that tells the declared percent and stat from every other percent in percentSpace and from every other stat, so a row this grid could not have caught is refused instead of shipped. A magnitude that scales per stack is replayed at every seed in stackSeeds, because the cap in that percent only bites on a unit that already holds stacks. Where two readings coincide the declaration follows the switch, which is why buff 2 says literal 10 even though 10 is also its rule secondary. statusPresentation is the battle HUD's identity table,